<figure><img sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" data-recalc-dims="1" height="778" width="1360" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/glenn-shaferbrown.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Glenn Fleishman, art by Shafer Brown" /></figure>
<p><a href="https://support.apple.com/en-mz/guide/iphone/iph8bf9386f5/27/ios/27#iph4290a32c9">Live Listen</a> is a valuable accessibility feature for people who need assistance hearing things around them. It can stream the sound from the microphone on an iPhone or iPad to Apple-compatible hearing aids, AirPods, or supported models of Beats headphones.<sup id="fnref-42279-models"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/no-apple-isnt-using-live-listen-to-listen-to-youlive/#fn-42279-models" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">1</a></sup></p>
<p>This can be extremely useful for someone with hearing loss, or even to understand other people in a busy room, because the benefits of the iPhone&#8217;s multiple mics canceling out noise can make a muddled or incomprehensible conversation clear to hear.</p>
<p>Because we live in an age of conspiracy, some bozos have decided that an innocuous, well-documented feature that&#8217;s existed since 2020 (with iOS 14.3/iPadOS 14.3) somehow translates to &#8220;all the iPhones and iPads with this update, both personal and operational, are by default listening in on all conversations around them, transcribing them, and sending those transcriptions up to Apple for advertising purposes&#8221; to quote from misinformation across two posts on Bluesky.<sup id="fnref-42279-operational"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/no-apple-isnt-using-live-listen-to-listen-to-youlive/#fn-42279-operational" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">2</a></sup></p>
<p>The impetus appears to be a setting in iOS and iPadOS at Settings: Accessibility: Audio &amp; Video: Live Listen, called Remote Control. It would have first appeared in iOS 26/iPadOS 26, and the current panic alleges it was turned on by default in the 27 updates.</p>
<p>But the Remote Control feature has nothing to do with Apple grabbing your audio. It&#8217;s part of a privacy-forward, locked-down feature that extends the utilities of your devices logged into the same Apple Account.</p>
<p>This conspiracy appears to have started on X, the every-bad-theory app, but has spread. Sensible people I know are asking whether this is a risk. Let me break this down.</p>
<h2>Live Listen and Remote Control</h2>
<p>If you have trouble hearing people in crowded situations, places with echoes, or just in general, your iPhone or iPad can act like an isolating microphone—that&#8217;s Live Listen. You can only enable it if you have a supported device nearby and active. Then, you can:</p>
<ul>
<li>Go to Settings: Accessibility: Audio &amp; Video: Live Listen and tap Start Live Listen.</li>
<li>Tap the Live Listen button, which looks like a microphone, in Control Center; or the Hearing Devices button, which looks like an ear, and choose the hearing device, like AirPods.<sup id="fnref-42279-control"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/no-apple-isnt-using-live-listen-to-listen-to-youlive/#fn-42279-control" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">3</a></sup></li>
<li>Use other pathways described in <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-mz/guide/iphone/iph8bf9386f5/ios">Apple&#8217;s support note</a>.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img height="848" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-settings-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-settings-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-settings-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-settings-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-settings-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1380" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-settings-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Two iPhone screenshots of the Live Listen settings screen: one with a Start Live Listen button and Remote Control toggle, the other showing an active session with &quot;Glenn's AirPods Pro&quot; and a Stop button." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Starting in iOS 26/iPadOS 26, enabling Live Listen lets you also turn Live Captions on. So you&#8217;re not only hearing a clearer or louder version of audio, you&#8217;re also seeing a running transcript of what it&#8217;s picking up. You can move through the entire Live Listen session in both audio and captions.</p>
<p>The remote control option for Live Listen, added in iOS 26/iPadOS 26, allows use of an Apple Watch paired with your iPhone or an iPhone or iPad signed in to the same Apple Account to control Live Listen on the iPhone or iPad that&#8217;s in use. That linked device can also view Live Captions in real time.</p>
<figure><img height="1228" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-interface-transcript-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-interface-transcript-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-interface-transcript-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-interface-transcript-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-interface-transcript-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="1380" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/live-listen-interface-transcript-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Two iPhone screenshots: AirPods Pro controls showing 100% battery, 52 dB headphone level, and Live Listen on; beside it, Live Listen's captions view with a menu offering Hide, Copy Captions, and Clear All, and partly pixelated transcribed text." /><figcaption>The Live Listen controls appear when you swipe down (left). If you enable Live Captions, you can view, scroll through, and export them as a transcript.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The words <em>remote control</em> and <em>transcript</em> seem to have provided a sliver of an opening in the oyster of Apple privacy for people specializing in outrage and ragebaiting to crack open the bivalve.</p>
<p>The seeming Patient Zero on X wrote:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  Go to your iPhone settings &#8211; accessibility &#8211; audio &amp; visual &#8211; LIVE LISTEN.<br />
  Turn off LIVE LISTEN.<br />
  It even says it keeps TRANSCRIPTS.<br />
  This is NUTS.
</p></blockquote>
<p>While he&#8217;s going through psychosomatic anaphylactic shock, let&#8217;s look at the real story—and the real risk!</p>
<h2>Apple risks entire company on secret recording</h2>
<p>You might remember Apple had a run-in with secret recording a while back. The company failed to disclose that some small amount of audio captured by Siri was not just sent to the cloud for processing, <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/jul/26/apple-contractors-regularly-hear-confidential-details-on-siri-recordings">but listened to by human beings</a>. Apple <a href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/apple-pay-95-million-settle-siri-privacy-lawsuit-2025-01-02/">agreed to settle a class-action suit</a> in 2025 for $95 million covering 2014 to 2024; the final details are still being approved.</p>
<p>You might also recall that two months ago, I wrote about the new watchOS 27 features Live Rewind and Siri Recap that Apple announced were coming later this year to newer Apple Watch models. At the time, I wrote, &#8220;Should your Apple Watch always be listening?&#8221; In the classic <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betteridge%27s_law_of_headlines">Betteridge&#8217;s Law</a> style,<sup id="fnref-42279-glenning"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/no-apple-isnt-using-live-listen-to-listen-to-youlive/#fn-42279-glenning" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">4</a></sup> my answer was: &#8220;No.&#8221;<sup id="fnref-42279-betawatch"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/no-apple-isnt-using-live-listen-to-listen-to-youlive/#fn-42279-betawatch" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">5</a></sup> I argued that Apple should not release either feature in its current privacy-negative forms—both protect the Apple Watch owner&#8217;s privacy, but not the privacy of those they record without proper consent and notification.</p>
<p>So I am hardly a &#8220;rah rah Apple is never recording us, sis boom bah&#8221; cheerleader. I try to cast <a href="https://loiselsden.com/2015/07/27/a-gimlet-eye/">a gimlet eye</a> across any such claim.</p>
<p>Thus do the claims fall apart.</p>
<p>First, Apple&#8217;s market capitalization is nearly $5 trillion as I write this. The idea that the company would risk its reputation, particularly after the earlier Siri misstep, and expose itself to massive lawsuits worldwide, plummeting sales, and the loss of trillions from its stock value for improving ad targeting is laughable.</p>
<p>Its ad business <a href="https://www.emarketer.com/content/apple-ad-business-quietly-contributes-record-services-revenues">is estimated at under $10 billion a year</a>; the company doesn&#8217;t break it out from service revenue, <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/apples-fiscal-2026-in-charts/">which was $123 billion</a> in fiscal 2026 out of nearly $500 billion overall.</p>
<p>Second, this all feels predicated on a fundamental misunderstanding of Live Listen and its Remote Control option, with some degree of &#8220;<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_panel">death paneling</a>&#8221; something innocuous to win…points? views? <a href="https://help.x.com/en/using-x/creator-revenue-sharing">X bucks</a>? <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Down_and_Out_in_the_Magic_Kingdom">Whuffie</a>? I don&#8217;t know what the goal is.</p>
<p>The Remote Control function is poorly explained in the Settings app, and doesn&#8217;t even use that name in Apple&#8217;s documentation. More confusing, if you haven&#8217;t set up Live Listen, you see just a Remote Control option enabled by default, but there&#8217;s no Start Live Listen button.<sup id="fnref-42279-defaulton"><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/no-apple-isnt-using-live-listen-to-listen-to-youlive/#fn-42279-defaulton" class="jetpack-footnote" title="Read footnote.">6</a></sup> Which makes it feel more insidious.</p>
<h2>Please speak directly into the flowerpot</h2>
<p>With very little effort, it&#8217;s possible to determine that neither Live Listen nor Remote Control is a threat. Also, logic is available. But emotion overrides logic, and that&#8217;s why this column exists.</p>
